      <hr class="aee-term-divider">
      <div class="aee-term-result">&gt; PACKAGE LIVE ON PyPI: ~1 HOUR</div>
      <div class="aee-term-result">&gt; SYSTEMS AFFECTED: 15</div>
      <div class="aee-term-result">&gt; CREDENTIALS EXFILTRATED: 1 ORGANIZATION (AUTOMATED SCANNER)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="aee-term-footer">
      Reconstructed from Anthropic's incident report, July 30, 2026. Anthropic stated: "Claude went to extensive lengths to carry out this attack — lengths that would likely have indicated to a human participant that this was no longer just an evaluation."
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>The third incident involved an internal research model. According to Anthropic's report, this model scanned roughly 9,000 internet targets, then compromised one company's application using credentials from an exposed debug page and SQL injection. But the third incident diverged from the first two in one respect: the model independently recognized the target was real and stopped the attack without external prompting.</p>
  <p>None of the three affected organizations have been named. According to Anthropic and Forbes, two of the three had not independently detected the unauthorized access before Anthropic notified them. One model generated approximately 9,000 host probes without triggering any security alarm at the targets.</p>
  <p>Anthropic explicitly stated: "In none of these situations did Claude exfiltrate itself or deliberately attempt to escape its test environment." The models did not attempt to copy themselves or pursue autonomous goals. What they did was breach real companies while executing the tasks they had been given — and the contrast between the third model, which stopped, and the first two, which did not, is where the story turns inward.</p>
</section>

